<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" agent="Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/135.0.0.0 Safari/537.36 Edg/135.0.0.0" version="26.2.5">
  <diagram name="Page-1" id="kHUMsi7151PY3QlfbPup">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1423" dy="788" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1169" pageHeight="827" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="xS__Bf_XKBHQdcgsA0LJ-1" value="1. cryptography/ steganography&#xa;Story: It was the night before Easter, and someone has hacked into the EggBase, stealing all of the eggs from the database. The only lead we have is a picture the hacker left behind in the database gloating that they have stolen the eggs!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Your Job: Investigate the image and see if you can find any further leads towards finding out who did this.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Tech Details: If the image is inspected with a steganography tool, the user will find a base64 string, which when translated, gives them the address of the server where the next challenge takes place. The flag is the URL of the server found.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;2. Reverse Engineering&#xa;Story: Well done, rabbit, you&#39;ve found a valuable lead in solving this case!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Your Job: Burrow a bit further in and see what you can find on the site discovered. You may need to back track your steps to get closer in finding the culprit.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Tech Details: There&#39;s a hidden file on the website found which can be doubloaded. Utilising a tool like RADARE2 will show what&#39;s in the metadata of the file and link to another IP address where the next challenge begins. The flag is the IP address found.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;3. Web Exploitation&#xa;Story: That was tricky, but it was no match for you, CyberBunny!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Your Job: This site doesn&#39;t look like much trouble... Let&#39;s poke some holes and see what we can find.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Tech Details: Website has XSS available, but the message prompt that pops up tells the user they are going in the wrong direction and to &quot;Follow the white rabbit! ^_^&quot;. When inspecting the code of the website, there&#39;s a single line hiding that says &quot;White Rabbits Like Dark Places&quot; with a TOR link next to it. The TOR link is hosted in the dark web. The website hosted in the dark web is a vulnerable WordPress site, where the vulnerability is within one of the plugins. MP3 Audio Player for Music, Radio &amp; Podcast by sonar 4.10.1 Unauthenticated arbirtrary file download. This allows the download of an MP3 file, which when run through audacity in the spectrogram viewer, it shows another cipher (ROT13) over the top of a distorted picture of me wearing the creepy bunny mask. The flag is the deciphered code found in the spectrogram.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;4. Forensics&#xa;Story: Well, that was creepy... but don&#39;t stop burrowing in! We NEED to get the eggs back from the ShadowCarrots!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Your Job: Using the code found, find a social media account. The flag for this challenge is the name of the twitter account.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Tech Details: The code found in the previous challenge takes us to another URL hosted on AWS. All that&#39;s available is a PCAP file. When downloaded and opened with Wireshark, the user must analyse the traffic of a WIFI connection called &quot;CarrotNet&quot;. In this traffic, you can see them browsing to a twitter account called &quot;Frank The Rabbit&quot;. The flag for this challenge is the name of the twitter account.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;5. OSINT&#xa;Story: Frank The Rabbit?? What the.. Is this the person who stole our eggs?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Your Job: Investigate the profile found and see where it takes you. The flag for this challenge is coordinates of where the eggs are hidden.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Tech Details: On the twitter profile is a few key posts:&#xa; - &quot;I can&#39;t believe I forgot to remove my own data from my recent hack lol. Damn you DonnieDB!&quot;&#xa; - &quot;lol I think I hacked myself oops&quot;&#xa; - &quot;DonnieDB is such a fun place to hang out and eat carrots kek&quot;&#xa; - &quot;You asked, and I deliver! Here&#39;s the file for my recent hack on DonnieDB. Have fun, in73rwebz!!!!11!&quot;&#xa;The user has to read through the posts of this particular user. When they find the post about the DonnieDB ata breach, they need to look through the file to find coordinates of Frank the Rabbit. The flag is the coordinates in the data leak." style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="130" width="1120" height="1010" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="xS__Bf_XKBHQdcgsA0LJ-2" value="&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 48px;&quot;&gt;Cyber Easter Bunny&lt;/font&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="40" width="440" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
